## 🧪 Self-Checking Testbench

A self-checking testbench is developed to verify RAM functionality automatically.

The testbench maintains an independent reference memory named `mem_cpy`, which stores the expected values for each memory location.

During every read operation, the actual RAM output is compared with the corresponding reference memory value.

### Testbench Features

- Independent reference memory.
- Automatic comparison of expected and actual data.
- Randomized write operations.
- Read verification across all memory locations.
- Error counter to track mismatches.
- Automatic PASS/FAIL reporting.

### Verification Logic

The testbench performs the following operations:

1. Generates stimulus for the RAM.
2. Drives data onto the shared bus during writes.
3. Updates the reference memory.
4. Reads the selected RAM address.
5. Compares the actual output against the expected output.
6. Increments the error counter whenever a mismatch occurs.
7. Displays the final verification status.

---

## 🔬 Test Sequence

| Step | Operation | Expected Result |
| :---: | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | Apply asynchronous reset | All memory locations become zero |
| 2 | Read all 8 locations | All locations return zero |
| 3 | Write random data into all 8 locations | Data stored successfully |
| 4 | Read all 8 locations | Actual data matches reference memory |
| 5 | Perform 20 random writes | Random data stored successfully |
| 6 | Read all 8 locations again | Data matches reference memory |
| 7 | Apply reset again | All memory locations become zero |
| 8 | Read all 8 locations | All locations return zero |

---

